<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Khanate of Kazan</h1><p class="st-meta">1438 CE – 1552 CE (approx.) · capital Kazan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Tatar khanate on the middle Volga, successor to Volga Bulgaria, whose conquest by Ivan the Terrible in 1552 opened the East to Russia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 1438 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a>, a deposed khan of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, settled in the former lands of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria</a> and established a khanate with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> as its capital. His son Mahmud consolidated it as an independent state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuvash-people">Chuvash</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mari">Mari</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/udmurts">Udmurts</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a>, with a farming and trading economy linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> route. The khan shared power with a council of leading princely clans and religious leaders. The city had mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and a strong merchant community. Rival factions favoured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> or the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate</a>, and Russian-backed khans such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-ali-khan">Shah Ali</a> were repeatedly installed.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After several failed campaigns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> besieged and stormed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in 1552, annexing the khanate to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Muscovy</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> population survived, and Kazan remained the centre of Tatar culture, now the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khanate-of-kazan.jpg" alt="Khanate of Kazan" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: HetmanTheResearcher · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banner_of_Kazan.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1438 CE – 1552 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kazan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ulugh Muhammad, Muhammad Amin, Söyembikä</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Kazan Tatar</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kazan Khanate, Qazan Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1495&amp;focus=khanate-of-kazan" aria-label="Khanate of Kazan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5750 276 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khanate of Kazan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6070 532L6352 637M6070 532L6076 554M6070 532L5820 533M6070 532L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6352 637h0M6076 554h0M5820 533h0M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 532h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kazan. <a href="/atlas?year=1495&amp;focus=khanate-of-kazan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
